Q: Why can't I declare C# methods virtual and static?
A: <p>It is possible to simulate the functionality by using the <strong>new</strong> keyword in the derived class and throwing the <strong>NotSupportedException()</strong> in the base.</p>
<pre><code>public class BaseClass{
    public static string GetString(){
        throw new NotSupportedException();   // This is not possible
    }
}

public class DerivedClassA : BaseClass {
    public static new string GetString(){
        return &quot;This is derived class A&quot;;
    }
}

public class DerivedClassB : BaseClass {
    public static new string GetString(){
        return &quot;This is derived class B&quot;;
    }
}

static public void Main(String[] args)
{
    Console.WriteLine(DerivedClassA.GetString());  // Prints &quot;This is derived class A&quot;
    Console.WriteLine(DerivedClassB.GetString());  // Prints &quot;This is derived class B&quot;
    Console.WriteLine(BaseClass.GetString());      // Throws NotSupportedException
}
</code></pre>
<p>Due to the fact that it is not possible to detect this condition at compile time and that IntelliSense won't suggest that such function should be implemented in the derived class, this is a potential headache.</p>
<p>One comment also suggested to use <strong>NotImplemetedException()</strong>. Microsoft's documentation indicates that neither of these exceptions should be handled so any of them should work.</p>
<p>The differences between <strong>NotSupportedException</strong> and <strong>NotImplemetedException</strong> are commented in <a href="https://blog.excastle.com/2004/10/15/notimplementedexception-vs-notsupportedexception/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this blog</a>.</p>

